# Understanding the DOM

#### The **DOM (Document Object Model)** is a bridge between a webpage’s code and what users see and interact with.
#### It’s a **programming interface** that turns the elements of an HTML document—like text, images, and buttons—into objects that JavaScript can access, modify, and respond to.

<br>

<img src="https://images.code.org/d77aa4ddfe768eb2e16d6072e8ac323f-image-1761850949297.png" alt="simple html map going from head to id of green button" width="350px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0 20px 0px 40px;">

#### Think of the DOM as a map or tree structure that shows how every element on a page is connected.
#### At the top is the document itself, which branches into sections (like `head` and `body`), and then into individual elements inside those sections.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

<br>

#### When JavaScript interacts with the DOM, it can select, listen to, and update these elements, allowing **structure (HTML), style (CSS), and behavior (JavaScript)** to work together as one unified, dynamic system.

---

